Feature: Data Profiling for Metric Tables in Unity Catalog

  Background:
    Given the Databricks environment is configured with Unity Catalog "purgo_databricks" and schema "purgo_playground"
    And the metric tables "health_insurance_claims_metric", "d_product_revenue_metric", and "s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric" exist in the schema
    And the output table "metric_profile" exists with the required schema

  # Happy Path: Profile all columns in all metric tables
  Scenario Outline: Generate data profile for each column in metric tables
    Given the metric table "<table_name>" contains column "<column_name>" of type "<data_type>"
    When the PySpark profiling script is executed
    Then a row is written to "metric_profile" with:
      | table_name   | column_name   | data_type   | null_count   | distinct_count   | total_count   | min_value   | max_value   | mean   |
      | <table_name> | <column_name> | <data_type> | <null_count> | <distinct_count> | <total_count> | <min_value> | <max_value> | <mean> |
    And the values for min_value, max_value, and mean are calculated as follows:
      | data_type | min_value rule | max_value rule | mean rule |
      | string    | lexicographical min | lexicographical max | null      |
      | bigint    | numeric min         | numeric max         | average   |
      | double    | numeric min         | numeric max         | average   |
      | int       | numeric min         | numeric max         | average   |
      | date      | earliest date       | latest date         | null      |
      | boolean   | min(0/1)            | max(0/1)            | average (as double) |
    And all values are cast to string except mean, which is double or null

    Examples:
      | table_name                                   | column_name                | data_type | null_count | distinct_count | total_count | min_value | max_value | mean   |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric               | Claim_ID                   | string    | 0          | 1000           | 1000        | A0001     | Z9999     | null   |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric               | Age                        | bigint    | 10         | 60             | 1000        | 18        | 99        | 45.2   |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric               | Service_Date               | date      | 0          | 365            | 1000        | 2023-01-01| 2023-12-31| null   |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric               | is_fraud                   | boolean   | 0          | 2              | 1000        | 0         | 1         | 0.03   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric                     | product_id                 | bigint    | 0          | 100            | 500         | 1         | 100       | 50.5   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric                     | product_name               | string    | 0          | 100            | 500         | Alpha     | Zulu      | null   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric                     | first_purchase_date        | date      | 5          | 365            | 500         | 2022-01-01| 2022-12-31| null   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric                     | customer_first_revenue     | double    | 20         | 200            | 500         | 10.0      | 1000.0    | 250.5  |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric| evenity_unit_hash          | int       | 0          | 8              | 100         | 1         | 8         | 4.5    |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric| pt_cdl_uuid                | string    | 0          | 100            | 100         | 0001      | 0100      | null   |

  # Error Path: Table does not exist
  Scenario: Profiling fails when metric table is missing
    Given the metric table "non_existent_metric_table" does not exist in the schema
    When the PySpark profiling script is executed
    Then an error is raised with message "Table non_existent_metric_table not found in purgo_databricks.purgo_playground"

  # Error Path: Column does not exist in table
  Scenario: Profiling fails when column is missing
    Given the metric table "health_insurance_claims_metric" exists
    And the column "NonExistentColumn" does not exist in the table
    When the PySpark profiling script attempts to profile "NonExistentColumn"
    Then an error is raised with message "Column NonExistentColumn not found in table health_insurance_claims_metric"

  # Error Path: Data type mismatch in metric_profile output
  Scenario Outline: Data type mismatch when writing to metric_profile
    Given the profiling result for "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" has value "<mean>" of type "<actual_type>"
    And the metric_profile schema expects "mean" as double
    When the script attempts to write the result
    Then an error is raised with message "Type mismatch for mean: expected double, got <actual_type>"

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | mean   | actual_type |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Gender        | null   | string      |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_name  | null   | string      |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | market_name | null | string |

  # Error Path: Duplicate profile rows for same column
  Scenario: Duplicate profile rows detected
    Given the profiling script generates multiple rows for the same table_name and column_name
    When writing to metric_profile
    Then an error is raised with message "Duplicate profile rows for table_name=<table_name>, column_name=<column_name>"

  # Validation: All required fields present in metric_profile
  Scenario Outline: Validate required fields in metric_profile output
    Given a profile row for "<table_name>" and "<column_name>" is written to metric_profile
    When the row is validated
    Then the following fields must be present and not null (except mean, min_value, max_value for non-numeric types):
      | table_name   | column_name   | data_type   | null_count   | distinct_count   | total_count |
    And for non-numeric columns, mean must be null

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | data_type |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Gender        | string    |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_name  | string    |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | market_name | string |

  # Validation: Allowed data types for profiling
  Scenario Outline: Validate allowed data types for profiling
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" has data type "<data_type>"
    When profiling is performed
    Then the data type must be one of: string, bigint, double, int, date, boolean
    And if not, an error is raised with message "Unsupported data type <data_type> for column <column_name>"

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | data_type |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Claim_ID      | string    |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_id    | bigint    |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | evenity_unit_hash | int |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Service_Date  | date      |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | is_fraud      | boolean   |

  # Validation: Null handling for mean/min/max
  Scenario Outline: Null handling for mean/min/max in non-numeric columns
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" is of type "<data_type>"
    When profiling is performed
    Then mean must be null for non-numeric types
    And min_value and max_value must be null for boolean columns

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | data_type |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Gender        | string    |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | is_fraud      | boolean   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_name  | string    |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | market_name | string |

  # Validation: Total count consistency
  Scenario Outline: Validate total_count matches table row count
    Given the metric table "<table_name>" has "<row_count>" rows
    When profiling is performed
    Then total_count for each column must be "<row_count>"

    Examples:
      | table_name                                   | row_count |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric               | 1000      |
      | d_product_revenue_metric                     | 500       |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric| 100       |

  # Validation: Distinct count calculation
  Scenario Outline: Validate distinct_count calculation
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" has "<distinct_count>" distinct values
    When profiling is performed
    Then distinct_count in metric_profile must be "<distinct_count>"

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | distinct_count |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Diagnosis_Code| 50            |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_id    | 100           |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | evenity_unit_hash | 8 |

  # Validation: Null count calculation
  Scenario Outline: Validate null_count calculation
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" has "<null_count>" null values
    When profiling is performed
    Then null_count in metric_profile must be "<null_count>"

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | null_count |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Age           | 10        |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | customer_first_revenue | 20 |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | market_name | 0 |

  # Validation: Min/Max calculation for string columns
  Scenario Outline: Validate min/max for string columns
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" is of type "string"
    When profiling is performed
    Then min_value is the lexicographically smallest value
    And max_value is the lexicographically largest value

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | min_value | max_value |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Claim_ID      | A0001     | Z9999     |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_name  | Alpha     | Zulu      |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | pt_cdl_uuid | 0001 | 0100 |

  # Validation: Min/Max calculation for date columns
  Scenario Outline: Validate min/max for date columns
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" is of type "date"
    When profiling is performed
    Then min_value is the earliest date
    And max_value is the latest date

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name           | min_value    | max_value    |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Service_Date          | 2023-01-01   | 2023-12-31   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | first_purchase_date   | 2022-01-01   | 2022-12-31   |

  # Validation: Mean calculation for numeric columns
  Scenario Outline: Validate mean for numeric columns
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" is of type "<data_type>"
    When profiling is performed
    Then mean is calculated as the average of non-null values

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | data_type | mean   |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Age           | bigint    | 45.2   |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | customer_first_revenue | double | 250.5 |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | evenity_unit_hash | int | 4.5 |

  # Validation: Mean is null for non-numeric columns
  Scenario Outline: Validate mean is null for non-numeric columns
    Given the column "<column_name>" in "<table_name>" is of type "<data_type>"
    When profiling is performed
    Then mean in metric_profile is null

    Examples:
      | table_name                       | column_name   | data_type |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Gender        | string    |
      | d_product_revenue_metric         | product_name  | string    |
      | s_field_reporting_sales_source_customer_metric | market_name | string |
      | health_insurance_claims_metric   | Service_Date  | date      |

  # END
